        ],
        "text": "John Howell v. Sandra Howell concerns the division of military retirement pay and whether a state court can provide indemnification for loss due to disability benefits."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. Wirken",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-6e75f3c88285-0001-0005"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 33,
              "start": 33
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Mr. Wirken, congress gave states the authority to divide retirement pay at the time of divorce, and the divided interests become vested, so an indemnification order does not violate the Act."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. Eisenstein (counsel)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "6e75f3c88285eb76b291cc736a9654a85423b26ae91bf61fceae20c89905673f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-6e75f3c88285-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 39,
              "start": 39
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Ms. Eisenstein (counsel), according to Ms. Eisenstein (counsel), the case also involves other assets, and the Arizona rule prohibits a party from unilaterally harming the other spouse\u2019s interest, allowing a makeup remedy."
      }
    ],
